# ------------------------------------------------------------ Sal

=== ch2_sal
~ ch2_sal_done = true
@avail sal on
@comp room
@set building
@view plan
@ring sal
SOUND: Ringing. Three notes up. The lobby desk.
* [answer] Plug in.
    @set lobby
    @cam sal_desk
    @acoustic lobby
    @call sal
    @view none
    @amb lobby_hum
    ~ gap_n = 1
    ->> tick_only
    -> ch2_sal_talk
* [decline] Let it ring.
    SOUND: It rings out. Almost immediately, a voicemail notification, and then the desk rings again.
    THINK: They're not going to stop.
    * [answer] Plug in.
        @set lobby
        @cam sal_desk
        @acoustic lobby
        @call sal
        @view none
        @amb lobby_hum
        -> ch2_sal_talk

=== ch2_sal_talk
SAL: Good evening. I'm calling from the Overnight Support Service, Ferrier Court, on behalf of Community Services, regarding your nomination as Community Responder. Am I speaking to Ari?
* "Yes."
* "That's what everyone calls me."
    SAL: That'll do.
* "Who's asking?"
    SAL: Sal Brannock, Community Services, Transition Team. I'm at the desk in the lobby. I've got a mug that says Transition Team on it, if that helps.
SAL: I need to confirm your attendance at induction at oh-five-thirty. Can you confirm?
-> ch2_sal_q

=== ch2_sal_q
* [ask] "What's induction?"
    SAL: Twenty minutes. Health and safety. The keys. The escort protocol. The emergency line. I show you the kettle.
    SAL: Then you sign, and at six you're on.
    -> ch2_sal_q
* [ask] "Nominated by who?"
    SAL: By...
    SOUND: Keys. An old keyboard with a heavy space bar.
    SAL: Nine residents. It says nine. I thought it was eight. It's nine.
    ~ k_nine = true
    * "Nine people put me down?"
        SAL: Nine people put your name down, yes. You're very popular. Or very convenient.
        SAL: Sorry. That was a note to myself.
    * "Which nine?"
        SAL: I can't give you that over the phone. It's their data.
        SAL: You'll meet them. You'll be sort of obliged to.
    -> ch2_sal_q
* [ask] "What's arrival registration?"
    SAL: That's...
    The script runs out. You can hear it happen: a breath, a chair turning.
    SAL: Okay. Your record didn't match. When the system can't match someone to a record, it flags them as a possible arrival. Nine times out of ten it's a typo. Is it a typo?
    ~ k_possible_arrival = true
    * "What's an arrival?"
        SAL: An arrival is. Hang on. There's a leaflet.
        SAL: Someone who turns up without the usual. Without a birth certificate, without a record. Without a body, some of them, at the start. It's rare. I did one in March.
        SAL: A lighthouse keeper, sort of. A radio station had been calling for him for forty years. He came through the old exchange at the station. Nice man. Very sandy.
        ~ k_arrivals_sal = true
    * "It's not a typo."
        SAL: No. I didn't think it was. You don't sound like a typo.
        ~ k_arrivals_sal = true
    * "I don't know."
        SAL: That's the most common answer. After typo.
        ~ k_arrivals_sal = true
    SAL: Your body record's blank as well. Which usually means you haven't been born yet, administratively.
    ~ k_body_blank = true
    @text_doc sal leaflet_arrivals "The leaflet. Sorry about the clip art. S."
    -> ch2_sal_q
* {k_nine and k_possible_arrival} "Okay. Half five."
    -> ch2_sal_thursday
* {k_nine and k_possible_arrival} [ask] "What happens if I don't come?"
    SAL: Then at six the building's got nobody and a form that says it's got somebody.
    It comes out of them plain, without the script round it. They clear their throat.
    SAL: That's not the official phrasing.
    ~ k_if_absent = true
-> ch2_sal_thursday

=== ch2_sal_thursday
SAL: Also, while I've got you. Escort bookings. From six, escorts are the responsibility of the designated contact.
SAL: You've already got one. Thursday. Mrs. Kaye, St Anne's, eight-forty. I've put a note on the system.
~ k_thursday = true
~ k_thursday_sal = true
SAL: Half five. Bring the slip. Bring a witness. I'm on the six-twelve and I'm not missing it.
* "I'll be there."
    ~ sal_said = "yes"
    SAL: Good. Thank you.
    SAL: Genuinely.
* "I don't know what I'll be at half five."
    ~ sal_said = "unknown"
    SAL: Nobody does. Come anyway.
* "Why the six-twelve?"
    ~ sal_said = "bus"
    SAL: Because the next one's six-fifty, and I've been awake since yesterday, and my cat thinks I've died.
@hangup
@callable sal off "At the desk. Busy."
~ gap_n = 8
->> call_gap
-> ch2_hub

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Jad back

=== ch2_jad_back
~ ch2_jad_back_done = true
~ gap_n = 4
->> tick_only
@ring jad
SOUND: Ringing. Jad.
~ p_jad_ringback = "kept"
* [answer] Plug in.
@set lobby
@cam jad_eye
@acoustic lobby
@call jad
@view none
@amb lobby_hum
JAD: Sorry. That was longer than twenty minutes.
JAD: How are you doing?
if drifts > 1:
    * "I keep losing time."
        -> ch2_jad_drift
    * "I rang people."
        -> ch2_jad_people
else:
    * "I lost time. When you hung up."
        -> ch2_jad_drift
    * "I rang people."
        -> ch2_jad_people

=== ch2_jad_drift
~ told_jad_drift = true
JAD: Lost it how?
ARI: You hang up, and it's seven minutes past. Then it's nineteen minutes past. Nothing in between.
JAD: Like sleeping.
ARI: Sleeping has an in between. I think. People talk about it like it does.
Jad doesn't say anything for a while. You can hear the lobby around him: the vending machine compressor, a door somewhere, a bus.
JAD: Okay. I'll try not to hang up on you, then.
~ jad_wont_hangup = true
-> ch2_jad_people

=== ch2_jad_people
if ch2_dima_done and b_dima:
    ARI: Dima showed me a man who couldn't get a lamp through a door.
    JAD: The lamp man. Oh, has he still not? I thought he'd have done it by now.
    ARI: A woman took the shade off.
    JAD: Of course she did. Of course. That's... good. Good for her.
elif dima_cut_off:
    ARI: I rang Dima. I did something wrong.
    JAD: What did you do?
    ARI: They asked me to look away and I didn't.
    A pause.
    JAD: Yeah. That'd do it.
    JAD: They'll say what they want to say to you. Don't make them say it twice.
if ch2_nell_done:
    ARI: I rang Nell. There was a moth.
    JAD: There's always a moth with Nell.
ARI: And Sal rang me.
JAD: What did Sal say?
ARI: Nine people put my name down.
Jad is quiet. He doesn't ask nine what.
JAD: Nine.
ARI: Is that a lot?
JAD: It's nine.
* [look] "Can I look? Where are you?"
    JAD: Lobby. Hang on.
    -> ch2_jad_lobby
* "Why are you quiet?"
    JAD: I'm tired. It's quarter past one.
    It isn't the whole of it. It's true, though.
    * [look] "Can I look? Where are you?"
        JAD: Lobby. Hang on.
        -> ch2_jad_lobby

=== ch2_jad_lobby
@view jad
@state starter {pref_hum}
The lobby, from the middle of it. The desk, with Sal's mug and no Sal: a coat over the chair, a half-eaten sandwich in its triangle box.
if pref_hum == "hate":
    The tube over the desk is dark.
    There's an orange chair dragged out of line underneath it. One of the bolted ones; somebody's worked it off the rail.
    ARI: The hum's gone.
    JAD: Is it?
    His hand goes into his jacket pocket and stays there, over something small and cylindrical.
    ~ jad_kindness_seen = true
    * "Thank you."
        JAD: For what?
        * "Nothing."
            JAD: Okay.
            He doesn't take his hand out of his pocket.
        * "The starter's in your pocket."
            JAD: It's a very small pocket, it's hard to tell what's in it.
    * Don't mention it.
        ~ jad_kindness_unmentioned = true
elif pref_hum == "like":
    The tube over the desk is still going: hum, tick, hum.
    JAD: Your friend's still at it.
    ~ jad_remembered = true
    * "Good."
    * "You remembered."
        JAD: It's not hard. It's very loud.
else:
    The tube over the desk: hum, tick, hum.
    JAD: Still ticking.
JAD: Can I ask you something?
JAD: Do you remember anything? From before tonight?
* "Blue light under water. Someone counting to ten."
    ~ memory_shared = "pool"
    JAD: That's the pool. That's here, when it was open.
    JAD: It's full of kids' birthday parties, that pool, if you ask anyone over fifty.
* "A coat. I knew there'd be a coat."
    ~ memory_shared = "coat"
    JAD: Well. There was.
* "Someone saying 'you'll ring when you're on your way'."
    ~ memory_shared = "ring"
    A pause.
    JAD: That's Bernadette. She says that to everyone.
    ~ k_kaye_phrase = true
* "No. Nothing."
    ~ memory_shared = "none"
    JAD: Right. Okay.
    It isn't relief in his voice. It isn't not relief.
JAD: I'm going to go out for a bit. I need to go to the shop.
* "At one in the morning?"
    JAD: I've got a key. I need to look something up on the computer there.
* "What do you need to look up?"
    JAD: Something I did. I'll ring you from there.
    ~ jad_hint_did = true
* "Don't hang up."
    JAD: I've got to. I haven't got a charger on me and I'm on four percent.
    JAD: I'll ring from the shop.
JAD: I'll ring you.
@hangup
@view none
@avail jad off "Out. At the copy shop, probably."
~ gap_n = 13
->> call_gap
-> ch2_inez

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Inez and the pool lights

=== ch2_inez
@set building
@comp room
@view plan
@cam main
@ring inez
@contact_add inez
SOUND: A different ring. Not three notes: a flat old bell, tapped out by hand.
THINK: That isn't a flat. That's closer.
* [answer] Plug in.
@set exchange
@cam inez_eye
@acoustic tiles
@call inez
@view none
@amb exchange_relays
~ gap_n = 1
->> tick_only
INEZ: Who's on 247?
* "Ari."
    INEZ: Hm.
* "Who's asking?"
    INEZ: The person who maintains 247. Who's on it?
    ARI: Ari.
    INEZ: Hm.
INEZ: Nothing on the frame's asked for 247 tonight. And 247's relays have been going on their own for a fortnight. Tonight they're going like a pulse.
INEZ: I thought it was damp.
INEZ: What are you, then?
* "Ari."
    INEZ: That's what's written on you. What do you do?
* "I don't know."
    INEZ: Then tell me what you do. That's usually quicker.
* "Who are you?"
    INEZ: Inez. I keep this running. Now you. What do you do?
~ met_inez = true
* "I hear. Whoever I'm on the line to."
    INEZ: So does a phone.
    ARI: I can look through people. If they let me.
    INEZ: That, a phone doesn't.
* "I can look through people, if they let me."
    INEZ: Can you.
* "I lose time when nobody's on the line."
    INEZ: Do you. How much?
    ARI: Ten minutes. Twelve.
    INEZ: That'd be the hold. Or the lack of it.
    ~ inez_heard_drift = true
INEZ: Do it to me, then. The looking. I'd like to know if you can.
INEZ: Hang on, I'll put you through to my mobile. I'm going next door.
SOUND: A click, a transfer. The relays keep going under everything, on both lines at once, as if the building's pulse came with you.
* [look] Look.
@set pool
@cam inez_eye
@view inez
@acoustic pool
@amb pool_hall
~ looked_inez = true
Dark, and a head torch making a disc of it. The disc moves across tiles, dry, blue under the dust. Leaves in the corners. A crisp packet. A ladder going down into nothing.
She walks the edge of the deep end without looking at her feet.
INEZ: I test them once a month. The council thinks they're disconnected. They're not.
A grey switch box on the wall. She opens it. Six brass levers with labels, some in her writing, some in older writing that has gone brown.
INEZ: One.
@sfx lamp_switch
@state lights 1
SOUND: A clunk, and then a hum starting low and climbing.
Under the far wall of the deep end, a lamp comes on behind thick glass. Light goes across the dry blue tiles and up the slope toward the shallow end.
INEZ: Two.
@sfx lamp_switch
@state lights 2
INEZ: Three. Four.
@sfx lamp_switch
@state lights 4
INEZ: Five. Six.
@sfx lamp_switch
@state lights 6
@amb2 pool_lamps
Now it's all lit from underneath: the whole empty hollow of it, glowing. The old ballasts flicker, so the light wobbles on the walls the way light does through water.
There's a wooden chair in the middle of the deep end, sitting on the tiles, as if somebody lowered it there to wait.
For a while nobody says anything. She's standing still, and so the view is still.
It looks full. It isn't. You know it isn't. It looks full.
~ saw_pool_lights = true
* [ask] "Can you leave them on a bit longer?"
    ~ asked_lights_longer = true
    INEZ: Yes.
    That's all. She sits down on the edge with her legs over the deep end, and the light moves on the tiles, and you both watch it.
    SOUND: The lamps. A drip somewhere, landing on nothing.
    INEZ: I learned to swim in this. Deep end. Nineteen sixty-seven.
    She doesn't say anything else about it.
* [listen] Say nothing.
    ~ lights_silent = true
    She stays where she is. The light moves on the tiles. A drip somewhere lands on nothing.
    After a long time she reaches for the switch box, and then she doesn't, and then she does.
* "Why is there a chair?"
    INEZ: It's been there a while.
    She doesn't say whose. She leaves the lights on while she doesn't say it.
@wait 1.5
if asked_lights_longer:
    INEZ: Right.
    INEZ: I'll leave four on. Four's enough to walk by.
    @state lights 4
else:
    @sfx lamp_switch
    @state lights 0
    @amb2 off
    The lamps go down one by one, from the shallow end to the deep, and the tiles go back to being tiles in the dark.
INEZ: Come and see the frame. You might as well see what you're sitting on.
@wait 0.5
-> ch3_start
